    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eCanned kidney beans are the shortcut worth taking.\u003c/strong\u003e Dried beans are cheaper per ounce, but they need a soak and an hour of babysitting. If you have the time, the dried bag saves real money on a batch this size.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eSmoked sausage is the flavor engine, so buy the rope.\u003c/strong\u003e Pre-sliced costs more per pound for a cut you can make in thirty seconds.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eOnion, celery, and bell pepper are the trinity and they are not optional.\u003c/strong\u003e Bell peppers are the priciest of the three, so buy them when the multi-packs are on sale and freeze the extras chopped.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eYour spice rack is doing most of the seasoning here.\u003c/strong\u003e Thyme, oregano, paprika, bay leaves, and a Cajun blend are jars you already own, and they cost a fraction of what a boxed red beans mix does."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Slice the smoked sausage into coins and brown them in the vegetable oil in a large pot, then set them aside.",
                    "Add the diced onion, celery, and green bell pepper to the same pot and cook until softened and the bottom of the pot picks up color.",
                    "Stir in the garlic, the dried parsley, dried thyme, dried oregano, paprika, and Cajun seasoning and cook for a minute so the spices bloom in the fat.",
                    "Add the kidney beans with their liquid and the chicken broth, then return the sausage to the pot.",
                    "Simmer uncovered for at least forty minutes, mashing some of the beans against the side of the pot to thicken the gravy.",
                    "Cook the rice separately according to the package while the beans simmer.",
                    "Fish out the bay leaves, season with salt and black pepper to taste, and serve the beans over the rice."
                ],
    "portion_html":  "Weigh the rice and the beans separately. Put each pot on the scale, subtract the empty-pot weight from Step 1, and divide by 14 so every container gets the same rice base and the same ladle of beans. Every serving lands near \u003cstrong\u003e{{cal}} calories with {{protein}} grams of protein\u003c/strong\u003e. The beans thicken overnight, so add a splash of water when you reheat.",
